        "title":  "Connecting AI Agents Was Only the Beginning. Now They Need to Think Together",
        "source":  "CIO.com",
        "url":  "https://www.cio.com/article/4222319/connecting-ai-agents-was-only-the-beginning-now-they-need-to-think-together.html",
        "date":  "2026-09-16",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "Authors from Outshift (Cisco\u0027s incubation group) describe the Internet of Cognition framework, which moves beyond agent connectivity to enable shared intent, shared memory, and coordinated reasoning across AI agents. Research cited identified a Communication-Reasoning Gap where connected agents exchanged messages but failed at collective computation. Outshift\u0027s Mycelium project improved agent alignment from 36% to 93% in testing using this framework.",
        "execSummary":  "The most significant finding for enterprise AI leaders is that connecting agents via APIs or protocols like Agent2Agent is necessary but insufficient—agents that can communicate still fail at collective reasoning without shared context and intent-alignment mechanisms. The jump from 36% to 93% alignment via the Mycelium framework quantifies the gap between wired-together and genuinely orchestrated AI systems, and should recalibrate expectations for multi-agent deployments currently in flight. For customer-support and operations leaders betting on agent networks to automate complex workflows, this research implies that architectural investment in cognition fabric—shared memory, negotiation, explainability—must accompany connectivity work. The human accountability layer is particularly relevant for regulated use cases where agent decisions must be auditable.",
